    68	## 2. Closed coefficient-array recurrence for `T_(t,k)`
    69	
    70	The coefficient-array identities in this section are **PROVED-HERE**.  They
    71	turn the charged equations (6.1)--(6.4) into indexed scalar arithmetic, rather
    72	than interpolation from fixed integers, and hide no monomial order.  The
    73	closed high-pivot formulas (2.10)--(2.12) are consumed from the proved Laurent
    74	spine in the charged Sol report, Section 5.1, equations (5.9)--(5.15).  This
    75	report rechecks their norms and fixed specializations; it does not relabel
    76	that banked symbolic derivation as new.
    77	
    78	Write `L=X-b4`, put `u_i=q_(i,0)`, and initially retain the high variables
    79	`u_t,...,u_(2t)` and `c_1,...,c_(t-1)`:
    80	
    81	```text
    82	U(X)=X^q + sum_(i=2)^(2t) u_i X^(q-i),
    83	C(X)=X^(t-1)+sum_(j=1)^(t-1)c_j X^(t-1-j).            (2.1)
    84	```
    85	
    86	Let `U_m=[L^m]U(L+b4)` and `C_m=[L^m]C(L+b4)`, with an array entry understood
    87	as zero outside its range.  Explicitly,
    88	
    89	```text
    90	U_m = binom(q,m)b4^(q-m)
    91	      +sum_(2<=i<=2t, q-i>=m)
    92	         u_i binom(q-i,m)b4^(q-i-m),
    93	C_m = binom(t-1,m)b4^(t-1-m)
    94	      +sum_(1<=j<t, t-1-j>=m)
    95	         c_j binom(t-1-j,m)b4^(t-1-j-m).             (2.2)
    96	```
    97	
    98	The polynomial called `T` in the compact remainder formula is denoted `B`
    99	here to distinguish it from the terminal rows.  Its integration constant is
   100	set to zero, and its remaining coefficients and the Euler solution `S` are
   101	
   102	```text
   103	B_0=0,
   104	B_(m+1)=g(3m+5)C_m/(2y(m+1)),  0<=m<=t-1,             (2.3)
   105	
   106	S_m={3g(m+1)U_(m+1)
   107	     +sum_(a+b=m-1)(3+2a-b)C_a B_b
   108	     +(g b3/2)(5m+7)C_m}/(y(2m+1)),  0<=m<=2t.       (2.4)
   109	```
   110	
   111	To verify (2.4), use `R_0=LC` in the charged Euler identity.  Its three
   112	bilinear terms combine as
   113	
   114	```text
   115	R_0 B-LR_0 B'+2LR_0'B=3LCB+2L^2C'B-L^2CB',
   116	```
   117	
   118	whose `L^m` coefficient is the convolution in (2.4); moreover
   119	`C+(5/2)(LC)'` contributes `(5m+7)C_m/2`.  This is a coefficient proof for
   120	every `m`, not a CAS pattern.
   121	
   122	Define
   123	
   124	```text
   125	V=L^2C-yb3,       Y=LS-b3B-gb2,       Z=LB-gb3,
   126	N=-VY'+V'Y+2U'Z,
   127	P0'=(N-N(0))/(2yL),
   128	R=VP0'-U'Y-yg=-E_t.                                  (2.5)
   129	```
   130	
   131	The displayed numerator `N-N(0)` is divisible by `L`.  The separate charged
   132	`D1` evaluation, already incorporated in (2.5), gives
   133	
   134	```text
   135	b1=-b3(U_1+b3 C_0)/y.                                 (2.6)
   136	```
   137	
   138	For completeness, changing the omitted constant `B_0` by `delta` changes the
   139	Euler right side by `delta(3LC+2L^2C')`; hence
   140	`delta S=(delta/y)LC`, `delta Y=(delta/y)V`, and `delta Z=delta L`.
   141	Consequently `delta N=2 delta L U'`, `delta P0'=delta U'/y`, and the two
   142	variations in `E_t=-VP0'+U'Y+yg` cancel.  Thus the terminal family is genuinely
   143	independent of `B_0`.  The charged gauge diagonal `q/y` is still a unit, but
   144	need not be computed to obtain the terminal rows.
   145	
   146	Here is the completely coefficientwise version of (2.5):
   147	
   148	```text
   149	V_m=C_(m-2)-yb3 delta_(m,0),
   150	Y_m=S_(m-1)-b3 B_m-gb2 delta_(m,0),
   151	Z_m=B_(m-1)-gb3 delta_(m,0),
   152	
   153	N_m=-sum_(a+b=m)V_a(b+1)Y_(b+1)
   154	    +sum_(a+b=m)(a+1)V_(a+1)Y_b
   155	    +2sum_(a+b=m)(a+1)U_(a+1)Z_b,
   156	(P0')_r=N_(r+1)/(2y),
   157	R_m=sum_(a+b=m)V_a(P0')_b
   158	    -sum_(a+b=m)(a+1)U_(a+1)Y_b-yg delta_(m,0).      (2.7)
   159	```
   160	
   161	All convolution indices in (2.4),(2.7) are nonnegative, and every array is
   162	zero outside its displayed polynomial range.
